Define the remaining order-to-int conversions in AMOS as x86-64 does.

amos/amos.h converts the order to int with `(int)fnu` (and once
`(int)(fnu + 0.5)`) at eleven sites besides the one patch 0002 rewrote.
For an order outside int range, or NaN, the conversion is undefined: x86-64
gives INT_MIN, arm64 saturates, and UBSan reports it. Orders of 2^31 or more
reach these sites through the complex Bessel functions (for example
iv(3e9, 1e9+1e9j), which is NaN+NaNj in SciPy 1.18.1 on x86-64).

zsp_order_int() returns INT_MIN outside int range and for NaN, and the plain
conversion inside it, so x86-64 is unchanged and every other platform now
computes what SciPy computes on x86-64. The `nui = (int)(fnul - dfnu) + 1`
site subtracts two bounded values and is left alone.

Upstream: https://github.com/scipy/xsf/issues/338

--- a/include/xsf/amos/amos.h
+++ b/include/xsf/amos/amos.h
@@ -99,6 +99,16 @@
 
 namespace xsf {
 namespace amos {
+
+    // Patched (zuspecial 0003): (int)fnu is undefined when fnu is outside
+    // int range or NaN. This gives what x86-64 gives there, INT_MIN, on
+    // every platform, so arm64 matches SciPy on x86-64.
+    inline int zsp_order_int(double fnu) {
+        if (fnu > -2147483649.0 && fnu < 2147483648.0) {
+            return static_cast<int>(fnu);
+        }
+        return -2147483647 - 1;
+    }
 
